//10. explain how virtual dom works and how it is coupled with state updates and state update API's



concept used in React to optimize updates and rendering of the UI. The idea is to create an in-memory representation of the actual DOM. This allows React to efficiently update only the parts of the DOM that need to change, rather than re-rendering the entire UI every time there is a change in state or props.

When a component first renders, React creates a Virtual DOM tree that mirrors the structure of the real DOM.

This tree is a lightweight JavaScript object that describes the structure of the UI.

When state or props change (via event handlers or other mechanisms), React triggers a re-render.

A new Virtual DOM tree is generated to reflect the updated state.

React compares the previous Virtual DOM tree with the new one (this process is known as "diffing").

It identifies the differences (or "diffs") between the old and new trees.
